.TH LORA 3
.SH NAME
lora \- addressed, acknowledged packets over a packet device
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind '#L' /net/lora
.PP
.B /net/lora/ctl
.br
.B /net/lora/addr
.br
.B /net/lora/stats
.br
.B /net/lora/clone
.br
.BI /net/lora/ n /ctl
.br
.BI /net/lora/ n /data
.br
.BI /net/lora/ n /local
.br
.BI /net/lora/ n /remote
.br
.BI /net/lora/ n /status
.br
.BI /net/lora/ n /listen
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I lora
device is the link layer that
.I radio (3)
deliberately does not have.  A packet device carries packets and knows
nothing about who they are for, the way
.I ether (3)
knows nothing about an IP address; everything that decides who a packet
is for, and whether it arrived, is here.
.PP
What goes on the air is the RadioHead header, four bytes of
.PP
.EX
	to from id flags
.EE
.PP
because that is what the other end of a PyCubed link speaks \-
.B lib/pycubed_rfm9x.py
and RadioHead's
.B RH_RF95
driver both put exactly these four in front of the payload.  Address 255
is broadcast, flag
.B 0x80
marks an acknowledgement and
.B 0x40
a retransmission.
.PP
The device is not welded to a radio.  It attaches to a file descriptor
written to its
.BR ctl ,
the way
.I ssl (3)
does.  It includes no header of the chip driver's: everything it knows
about what is underneath it, it learns from the channel.
.PP
What is underneath must delimit messages and must publish an
.BR iounit ,
which is a real requirement and not a style.  A pipe does neither \-
.B os/inferno/port/devpipe.c:73
opens its queues as byte streams \- so a pipe will not carry this, and
the only thing in the tree that will is a packet device.
.PP
.B Ctl
takes:
.TP
.BI fd " n"
Attach the packet device open on file descriptor
.IR n .
The device keeps its own reference, so the process that wrote this may
close the descriptor and exit.  Once only, and for the life of the
boot: there is no detach, and the reader process this starts holds the
packet device's receive path from then on.  On
.I radio (3)
that is a claim, so
.B radiochk recv
and anything else that reads
.B /dev/radio0/data
directly is refused with a message saying why rather than blocking on
packets it will never see.
.TP
.BI addr " n"
This station's address, 0 to 254.
.PP
.B Addr
reads back that address and
.B stats
the interface's counters.
.PP
Opening
.B clone
makes a conversation and returns its
.BR ctl ,
whose first read is the conversation's number.  Holding that descriptor
open is what holds the conversation open; closing it closes the
conversation, as on
.IR ip (3).
A conversation's
.B ctl
takes:
.TP
.BI connect " n"
Send to address
.IR n ,
and take packets from it.
.TP
.B announce
Take packets from any address no other conversation has claimed.  An
announced conversation does not carry any itself; see
.B listen
below.
.TP
.B ack on
.RB | off
Send reliably, and acknowledge what arrives.  Off by default, because a
link with one end acknowledging and the other not is worse than neither.
.TP
.BI retries " n"
How many times a reliable send is attempted before it gives up.
.TP
.BI timeout " ms"
How long to wait for each acknowledgement.  A retransmission follows a
random backoff of up to half that again, so two stations that collided
once do not collide identically for ever; an acknowledgement arriving
during the backoff still ends the send.
.PP
Opening
.B listen
on an announced conversation waits for a station nobody has claimed to
say something, and hands back that station's conversation \- a
different one, whose number is its first read, with the packet that
made it already queued.  That is
.I ip (3)'s
shape and
.I ax25 (3)'s,
and it is what makes two stations talking to one server two
conversations rather than one whose remote address changes under the
reader.  An announcer that closes takes with it any conversation it
made and nobody took.
.PP
.B Data
is one packet per read and one per write, inheriting both of
.IR radio (3)'s
refusals: a read whose buffer is smaller than the packet fails and the
packet stays, and a write past the payload budget fails and names it.  A
write with nothing to send to \- a conversation that has neither
connected nor been spoken to \- fails rather than broadcasting, because
a silent broadcast is not what anyone meant.
.PP
The
.B iounit
of
.B data
is the packet device's less the four header bytes.  It is republished
rather than invented: a filter above lands on a number that accounts for
a header it never sees, which is how a Styx server over a radio
negotiates a radio-sized message without being told there is a radio.
A packet device that publishes no usable iounit is refused at
.BR "fd n" ,
because guessing would put the error at the far end of a link.
.PP
With
.B ack
on, a write is a reliable datagram in the manner of
.IR lib/pycubed_rfm9x.py 's
.BR send_with_ack :
the identifier carries a sequence number, the sender waits for a packet
with the ACK flag and the same identifier, and a retransmission sets the
RETRY flag.  A receiver replies to anything addressed to it that is not
itself an acknowledgement, with the payload
.BR ! ,
and it does so from a separate process, because a reply is a transmit
and a transmit blocks for the packet's whole time on the air \- over a
second at SF12, during which nothing would be read.  A broadcast is
neither acknowledged nor waited for.
.PP
A retransmission of something already delivered means the
acknowledgement was lost rather than the packet, so it is acknowledged
again and not delivered again.  The last identifier from each address is
kept for that, flat, 512 bytes, as RadioHead keeps it.  Being a
duplicate is decided before anybody is looked for to receive it,
because it is a fact about the link and not about a conversation.
.PP
.B Timeout
has to be longer than the other station owes its duty cycle, and
nothing here can work that out.  A station that has just spent
.I A
milliseconds on the air, at a duty cycle of
.I D
per cent, cannot answer anything for
.I "A (100 - D) / D"
afterwards, however little the acknowledgement itself costs.  One-way
traffic composes without thinking about it, because an acknowledgement
is much shorter than the packet it answers and the sender's own gap
covers it; two-way traffic does not.  Measured, with the answering
station at 2 per cent and the sender at 5: a station that had just put
a 200-byte packet of its own on the air owed about sixteen seconds of
silence, the sender's four-second
.B timeout
expired twice, and the acknowledgement arrived on the third attempt.
.B "sent 3, retried 2, ackrecv 1"
at one end and
.B "duplicate 2, acksent 1"
at the other \- the two retransmissions recognised and not delivered
twice \- and the write took fourteen seconds to return.
.PP
A write returns the number of bytes sent, or fails, and with
.B ack
on it fails only after every retry has been tried.  That is the
difference between this and an unacknowledged write, which cannot fail
for any reason the other end would know about.
.PP
.B Stats
reports whether a packet device is attached, this station's address, the
iounit underneath and the payload above it, packets in and out,
.RB ( notours )
heard but addressed elsewhere,
.RB ( runt )
too short to carry a header,
.RB ( noconv )
for this station with no conversation to take them,
.RB ( acksent )
acknowledgements sent,
.RB ( accepted )
conversations made for a station nobody had claimed,
.RB ( duplicate )
retransmissions recognised and not delivered twice, and
.RB ( ackdropped )
acknowledgements owed and lost because too many were owed at once.
.PP
A conversation's
.B status
reports its state, whether it announced, the address at the other end,
its acknowledgement settings, and its own counters.
.B Local
and
.B remote
are those two addresses on their own.
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/inferno/radio/devlora.c
.SH BUGS
There is no
.B listen
and no
.BR accept ,
so an announced conversation serves one peer at a time: a second station
talking to it interleaves, and a reply goes to whichever spoke last.
Two stations that must both be served want two conversations and a
.B connect
each.
.PP
Four conversations, which is a constant.
.PP
Nothing here arbitrates the air between stations, because nothing below
it does either; see the same entry in
.IR radio (3).
Acknowledgement and retry make that worse rather than better, since a
lost acknowledgement puts a second copy of a packet on a band that was
already busy.
.PP
.B Timeout
is a constant with a default of two seconds, and by the paragraph above
it should be derived from what the other station owes.  Deriving it
would mean this layer knowing the modem's parameters, which is the
coupling it exists to avoid, so it is left to whoever sets it and
written down here instead.
.PP
There is no detach.  A packet device attached to this is attached until
the next boot.
.PP
The address is a byte, which is RadioHead's and therefore a PyCubed's.
It is not a name, there is no way to learn one, and two stations
configured with the same address will both answer.
